\section{The ABI is only one part of the boundary}

An interface to another runtime needs agreement on value encoding, ownership, thread entry, and control transfers. Matching C argument registers does not say whether a retained pointer remains valid after collection, or whether an asynchronous callback is permitted to enter the runtime on a worker thread. A boundary type should specify whether an object is copied, borrowed, retained by a handle, or pinned. Also state how calls distinguish normal return, foreign failure, and Scheme exceptions. If there is a representation whose lifetime depends on a particular thread or dynamic call, encode that restriction in the interface rather than leaving it to individual callers to infer.

Marcus Crestani's \emph{Foreign-Function Interfaces for Garbage-Collected Programming Languages} compares mechanisms including explicit protection of variables and reference objects for Scheme 48. A moving collector must be able to find and update references to objects it moves. It cannot in general find an arbitrary copy of a raw pointer in foreign storage. A handle allows the runtime to update a known root slot while C retains a stable reference to the slot. Copying arguments avoids that coupling but may lose shared identity and duplicate large data. Pinning can preserve the original storage address, at the cost of restricting collector movement while the pin is held.

A useful static check is to mark foreign calls that might indirectly enter the collector. Michael Furr and Jeffrey S.\ Foster's \emph{Checking Type Safety of Foreign Function Calls} develops this idea for OCaml-to-C, not for Scheme. Adapt the premise to a Scheme wrapper by marking allocating calls and callbacks as possible collection points, then requiring that Scheme references live across those calls be in locations the collector knows. An API wrapper that itself does not allocate can still inherit that obligation from a callback.

\section{Worked boundary: retain and then invoke a callback}

Suppose a Scheme procedure registers a callback with a C timer which will fire after the registration call returns. Give the registration record an owning handle to the closure, rather than a call-local reference. The collector traces the slot and updates it if the closure moves. When a timer event occurs, acquire a live reference to the registration, enter the Scheme runtime using the supported thread-entry mechanism or enqueue the event on its scheduler, and dereference the handle in that context. Marshall the timer's event argument there. On cancellation, coordinate retirement with any in-flight callback before releasing the handle. The registration may have ended for the API caller while one callback still has a right to run.

Test by collecting between registration and invocation and by forcing allocation during the callback. Then race cancellation against a pending invocation. Each invocation must either hold a usable reference until it exits or be rejected before dereferencing the handle. An implementation that releases the handle on cancellation but allows an already scheduled worker to dereference it violates this invariant. The callback wrapper also needs a policy for exceptions and continuations: a resumable Scheme continuation must not implicitly promise to reinstate a foreign frame that has already unwound.

\section{JavaScript and managed code draw different lines}

Marc-Andr\'e B\'elanger and Marc Feeley's \emph{A Scheme Foreign Function Interface to JavaScript Based on an Infix Extension} describes Gambit's mostly implicit conversions and direct-style integration between Scheme threads and JavaScript promises. The invoking Scheme thread can wait for a promise without blocking the host scheduler. A JavaScript completion callback conveys fulfillment or rejection back to Scheme. Test both paths while a second Scheme thread makes progress. Implicit conversion is also not a promise of lossless round trips: for instance a Scheme exact integer beyond JavaScript's safe integer range need not survive conversion to a JavaScript number and back.

Yannis Bres, Bernard Paul Serpette, and Manuel Serrano's \emph{Compiling Scheme Programs to .NET Common Intermediate Language} reports a historical Bigloo backend with managed closure representations and CIL tail-call support. Tail transfer on the host does not provide stack capture. The reported managed backends use an exception-based mechanism limited to the dynamic extent of capture, not full R5RS continuations. A separate Scheme stack would make more control state representable, but would require a protocol for callbacks and foreign invocation. Test long tail calls and resumption after an earlier call has returned as different properties. The dossier's ABI discussion in Chapter~\ref{ch:adaptive-abi} concerns agreement on how to communicate; this example shows why an adapter must also respect what control and lifetime semantics each side can actually supply.
